 2. 🗳️ Mail-in maelstrom 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Supreme Court cleared the way yesterday for President Trump to implement parts of an executive order restricting mail-in ballots.

 Why it matters: The decision could complicate the midterms, putting election officials on a tight deadline to comply with potential changes, Axios' Josephine Walker and Avery Lotz write. Catch up quick: Trump's order, issued in March, directed the Department of Homeland Security to compile lists of citizens eligible to vote and, separately, the U.S. Postal Service to craft rules restricting ballots to those on state-submitted lists of eligible voters.

 A coalition of Democratic state attorneys general challenged it , arguing that states administer elections under the Constitution . 🏛️ SCOTUS didn't decide whether Trump's order was legal, instead finding that states lacked standing to challenge the executive order because it was an "internal directive" that didn't harm the states that sued.

 The ruling was 6-3, with the three liberal justices dissenting. What's next: The USPS is still blocked from implementing its rule changes for now due to a nationwide injunction in a separate case earlier this month. 

