 3. 📝 Data center cheat sheet 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A special look for AM readers at Axios CEO Jim VandeHei's deep dive on data centers — suddenly animating the midterms — published first in his weekly Axios C-Suite newsletter: 

 It's almost impossible to wrap your brain around the scale of the cash dedicated to the data center buildout — now the biggest capital project in human history. The key stats:

 📈 Total online: About 4,000 and counting across the U.S., with 580 hyperscale facilities designed to handle the massive computing demands of tech giants. 🗳️ Total delayed by politics: At least 75 projects nationwide totaling roughly $130B in potential investment were impacted in Q1 2026. 💡 Average site capacity: 62 megawatts — nearly twice the power capacity of the typical site in Virginia at the end of 2024. 💰 Average cost to build: Roughly $2.1B-$2.4B all in for a 62-megawatt data center. 🧑‍💻 Average employees: About 50 to keep the average Virginia site running. That number balloons to a peak of 1,500 workers onsite during the 12–18 months of construction. Hyperion, Meta's largest data center, is under construction in Richland Parish, La. Photo: Meta Between the lines: Yes, data centers guzzle both water and power, but one demand dwarfs the other.

 Water, overhyped: The water use of every data center in America in 2023 equaled less than 1% of the water used to irrigate the nation's agriculture that year. Electricity, underhyped: Their electricity use is likely more massive than you imagine — and still surging. Data centers used 4.4% of U.S. electricity in 2023. That's forecast to be 12% by 2030. Read on. 

 📈 If you're a CEO or on a CEO's team: Ask to join Jim's new weekly Axios C-Suite newsletter. 
 
